          "documents": [
            {
              "type": "Discharge Summary",
              "title": "Hospital Discharge Summary",
              "author": "hospital medicine",
              "date": "2025-08-22T10:00",
              "body": "Regional Hospital - Internal Medicine Discharge Summary\nAdmitted: 08/14/2025   Discharged: 08/21/2025\n\nPrincipal diagnosis: severe alcoholic hepatitis.\nOther: alcoholic cirrhosis; chronic pancreatitis; right upper lobe NSCLC s/p SBRT 2024; aortoiliac occlusive disease; COPD; alcohol use disorder, severe; tobacco use.\n\nPresentation: brought in by his wife with weeks of worsening fatigue and jaundice that had become much deeper over the last several days. Biopsy-proven alcoholic hepatitis in February 2025. Continued daily drinking.\n\nCourse: Transaminases and bilirubin elevated on arrival. Total bilirubin peaked at 8.4 mg/dL; PT 18.4 seconds (control 12.0), Maddrey discriminant function 38. Infection was excluded before steroids: blood and urine cultures negative. Prednisolone 40 mg daily was started 8/15 for a planned 28-day course. Day-7 Lille score 0.31, consistent with response, so steroids were continued. Monitored for withdrawal; no complications. Nutrition consulted. Abstinence counseling given; he expressed willingness to stop.\n\nDischarge medications: prednisolone 40 mg daily through 9/11/2025 (then stop, no taper); albuterol HFA prn; ipratropium HFA QID; pancrelipase 36,000 units with meals and snacks; omeprazole 20 mg daily; aspirin 81 mg daily; atorvastatin 40 mg daily; thiamine 100 mg daily; folic acid 1 mg daily; multivitamin daily.\nAllergies: NKDA.\n\nFollow-up: PCP within 1 week with CMP, CBC, INR; gastroenterology in 4 weeks. Complete alcohol abstinence."
            }
