            {
              "type": "ED Notes",
              "title": "Emergency Department Record",
              "author": "emergency medicine",
              "date": "2026-02-11T08:00",
              "body": "Regional Hospital Emergency Department\nDate of service: 02/09/2026\n\nChief complaint: yellow skin, swollen belly, feeling unwell for 2 weeks.\n\nHPI: 55-year-old man with alcoholic cirrhosis, prior severe alcoholic hepatitis (steroid-treated 8/2025), chronic pancreatitis, treated right upper lobe lung cancer, aortoiliac occlusive disease and COPD. Over two weeks his jaundice has steadily deepened, his abdomen has swelled and he has felt generally unwell. He says he has been drinking a fifth of vodka a day for the past ten years.\n\nMeds: per PCP list (inhalers, Creon, omeprazole, aspirin, atorvastatin, thiamine, folic acid, multivitamin). NKDA.\n\nExam: afebrile, vitals as charted. Scleral icterus. Abdomen distended with a fluid wave, consistent with ascites. Liver palpable 3 cm below the right costal margin; mildly tender in the right upper quadrant. No peripheral edema. Alert and oriented.\n\nLabs: AST 220 IU/L, ALT 90 IU/L, GGT 300 IU/L, total bilirubin 5.2 mg/dL, direct bilirubin 3.1 mg/dL, albumin 2.9 g/dL, PT 18 s (control 12 s), ferritin 900 ng/mL.\n\nMDM: decompensated alcoholic liver disease with new ascites and recurrent alcoholic hepatitis. Needs diagnostic paracentesis to exclude infection and inpatient care.\n\nDisposition: admitted to hospital medicine."
            }
